it's great to be joined today by John  kinder Fogg who joined Palo Alto  Networks as field CTO after eight and a  half years at Forrester Research where  he was a vice president and principal  analyst on the security and risk team  considered one of the foremost  cybersecurity experts in the world very  well known for creating the zero trust  model of cybersecurity which we'll talk  about so John we recently spoke to Molly  McHugh about information warfare and  sort of some ancillary or tangential  issues that are related to cyber  security in many ways let's start maybe  more broadly with when we talk about  cyber security what are we talking about  and what are the sort of recent concerns  in the field  well cyber security is becoming an all  incomes encompassing term for bad things  that happen on networks whether they're  public networks or private networks and  how do you secure the systems and the  data the devices all that kind of stuff  so it's a very broad term that it could  include things like cyber warfare all  types of hacking data breaches so it's  it's not very specific but it does seem  to resonate with people so they  understand that the term is about the  cybers right we're not trying to protect  the cyber as we're trying to protect  data we're trying to protect assets  though what are the sort of day-to-day  ways in which the average user of  Technology you have a smartphone you  probably have it connected to both  social networks and your email you're  regularly browsing websites on it you  have some you know regular computer use  as part of your job as well as  personally what are the biggest  cybersecurity sort of concerns that  would impact the average person in that  way here's the deal David I don't think  that the average person can really be  concerned about cybersecurity and live  their life I think that we spend too  much time blaming the victims of  cybersecurity intent instead of trying  to fix the fundamental  problems in the systems that allowed  these kinds of you know negative  consequences of using technology to  happen so I'm not I'm not a person who's  going to give people a lot of advice on  their home usage I mean there's a few  things that are that are pretty simple  don't use an unlocked cell phone for  example get your get your applications  from the store of the you know the phone  manufacturer because at least they've  done some vetting of the technology and  every once in a while something will  slip by but think of the store as a  whitelist for the applications you  should use those are some of the simple  things but the rest of it is way too  complicated even for the the top people  to always protect themselves let alone  the average user who just wants to get  their day-to-day job done so is  cybersecurity really at more of an  institutional issues when we're talking  about governments and and corporations  that are providers of either hardware or  software connectivity is that really  where the crux of the issue is  absolutely those are the people who are  going to have to do it right you you  just it's too complex for us to push it  all all the way to the edges of the user  so I liken it to trying to decide  whether or not to go into a building if  it's safe or not you know do you look  and try to figure out well should I go  into this restaurant will the building  fall down while I'm eating lunch no  that's the job of some other profession  in that case you know the civil  engineering and code enforcement and all  that kind of thing and so that's what  eventually cybersecurity is going to  have to be it's going to have to be the  civil engineering and the code  enforcement of of the Internet of  connectivity to make sure that people  can use this technology in a safe and  secure fashion before we move into  talking about zero trust based on your  observation of the way in which  technology is developing what  on the horizon in terms of the types of  cyber security concerns that may surface  which were not even necessarily dealing  with right now is there anything you can  sort of paint a picture for us of  regarding in terms of on the horizon  well the horizon is is limitless from  the the bad guys and girls perspective I  mean where we're gonna see it a lot of  it is in the so-called Internet of  Things your your smart thermostat we've  seen it with the IP cameras that have  been used in the mirai botnet anything  that exists technologically people with  malicious intentions are going to try to  abuse that technology for their own  purposes and we should never  underestimate the threats just because a  threat doesn't exist today doesn't mean  that it can't exist tomorrow and and and  it's limited by the the by the  imagination of the adversary this is an  adversarial business and so we're always  a little bit behind if you're you're on  the good guy the good girl side  are there any institutions or  organizations that that really seem to  be sort of throw actively at the maximum  point they could be and I guess to sort  of contextualize that we've we saw so  many cyber security breaches during 2016  we you know everything from the Justice  Department and the IRS snapchat Yahoo  and 500 million accounts or something  like that I mean is there a type of  institution that seems to be better  protected I don't know if there's a type  it's it's an attitude and it's a it's an  Enlightenment you know Yahoo they used  to call the the security people the  paranoids  oh you're paranoid you're just you know  that will never happen to us only  because we couldn't imagine it happening  to us but it did happen to them and it  cost  Yahoo almost the entire value of its  company so you just have to realize that  this is in a different landscape a  different world it doesn't  world isn't at all like the analog world  and all of the the analogies that we try  to do to make it understandable to us  just don't work you know you're you're  living in the same bad neighborhood is  every single attacker on the world  there's no suburbs on the internet you  can't hide and so you're going to have  to protect yourself let's talk a little  bit about the zero trust model that you  developed what is that for people who  aren't familiar with it let's start  there the zero trust model is a a new  model of cybersecurity that says that  the old model where users and networks  and devices inside of a of a perimeter  inside of a company or an organization  are trusted and everything outside of  that organization is untrusted and  therefore we only have to protect from  attacks coming from the outside and and  the mantra was originally trust but  verify which everybody says well Ronald  Reagan said that and he actually didn't  he was making a joke where he he uses a  Russian proverb  speaking in Russian and says oh that of  course is translated as trust but verify  and everybody laughs when he gives this  speech because they know the essence of  the Russian proverb is not trust but  verify but never trust at all and so  when I started looking at at what was  the fundamental problem the fundamental  problem is that we embedded trust into a  system that not only doesn't need trust  to function by functions poorly because  we add trust to it interesting and from  a practical perspective what are some of  the sort of bullet points of how this  functions well the first thing is we  need to understand that no people are  ever on networks so people say you're  saying that people aren't trustworthy  and I'm not saying that that's a that's  a discussion issue you should go back  and have with Julius Caesar I'm saying  that people aren't packets no person  who's ever been on a network you and I  David we're not on the network right now  there are packets traversing networks  that are  to come from identities that may or may  not belong to us but we're not on the  network so we're not doing anything to  impugn people's trustworthiness we're  just saying we're going to treat each  and every packet exactly the same and  when we do that that just opens up a lot  of doors and a lot of Revelations and so  we can we can inspect all the traffic we  can log all the traffic we can put much  more granular controls on it and most  importantly we start at the thing we're  trying to protect I used the analogy of  the u.s. Secret Service The Secret  Service knows three things that we don't  know in cybersecurity they know who the  president is they know where the  president is and they know who's  supposed to act access to the president  if I asked almost any corporation about  the data or assets that they need to  protect they can't tell me those three  things they don't know what to protect  they don't know where the things that  they're supposed to protect are and they  don't know who's supposed to have access  to the things that they're supposed to  protect  therefore they just push all the  controls to some edge and hope that  maybe they catch something bad happening  before it's too late last thing I want  to touch on there are some who say that  businesses sort of have a free market  natural incentive to regulate themselves  in a way that will make them not  problematic or dangerous to employees  customers the environment etc and in my  view is that that's been pretty widely  debunked when it comes to corporations  and cybersecurity does such a natural  interest exist to protect all data  customers employees etc or does that not  exist and it would need to sort of be  mandated by some kind of regulatory  apparatus it should exist because all  regulations and compliance in my view is  a failure of corporate governance we  didn't get ahead of the problem and so  there will always be more and more  governance added to it and until people  realize that it you know security cyber  security is a pay me now or pay me a lot  more later kind of business  and if you understand that then maybe  those incentives can can be in place  secondly though and I think the big  thing that's that's changing is that  CEOs are and leaders of organizations  are getting fired because of data  breaches we saw that with target that  was the first one that really put a  shockwave through the entire industry  because suddenly it wasn't just oh I'm  gonna fire the security guy who didn't  do a good job even though I didn't give  him a budget to do a good job it was oh  the board has to protect themselves so  they're gonna get rid of the leader and  whether that's the CEO of Target or  whether that's Katherine Archuleta of  the OPM Office of Personnel Management  or or the head of Sony I mean there's  just so many times that people have  gotten fired the leaders of companies or  organ organizations have gotten fired  and so that does change the incentive  structure pretty pretty strongly but  there still will always be regulations  because we're always going to be cheap  that's just human nature  indeed it is we've been speaking with  John kinder vog Palo Alto Networks field  CTO thanks so much for talking to us  about this thank you very much David

